\ The Pastafarian Calendar: exact arithmetic with long, nonnegative numbers.
\ Every arithmetic routine here is Plain English Programming.
\ The digits are decimal text, with no leading zeroes except for "0".
\ All callers must give nonempty strings containing only ASCII digits.
\ This is a source implementation. CAL compilation has not yet been verified.

A wide quantity is a string.

\ Taking the final digit also removes it from the given text.
\ An empty string supplies a zero digit, useful when adding numbers.
To take the last decimal digit from a string into a number:
  Put 0 into the number.
  If the string is blank, exit.
  Put the string's last's target into the number.
  Subtract 48 from the number.
  Remove the last byte from the string.

\ Turn a small number from 0 to 9 into its printable decimal character.
To prepend a decimal digit number to a string:
  Put the decimal digit number into another number.
  Add 48 to the other number.
  Put the other number into a byte.
  Prepend the byte to the string.

\ Keep one zero if all of the digits were zero.
To normalize a wide quantity:
  If the wide quantity is blank, put "0" into the wide quantity; exit.
  Loop.
    If the wide quantity's length is 1, break.
    If the wide quantity's first's target is not the zero byte, break.
    Remove the first byte from the wide quantity.
  Repeat.

\ Compare two positive or zero whole numbers, not their textual spelling.
\ A shorter normalized decimal number is always smaller.
To decide if a wide quantity is less than another wide quantity:
  If the wide quantity's length is less than the other wide quantity's length, say yes.
  If the wide quantity's length is greater than the other wide quantity's length, say no.
  Put the wide quantity into a left string.
  Put the other wide quantity into a right string.
  Slap a substring on the left string.
  Slap another substring on the right string.
  Loop.
    If the substring is blank, say no.
    If the substring's first's target is less than the other substring's first's target, say yes.
    If the substring's first's target is greater than the other substring's first's target, say no.
    Add 1 to the substring's first.
    Add 1 to the other substring's first.
  Repeat.

\ Add the first value into the second; the first is not changed.
To add a wide quantity to another wide quantity:
  Put the wide quantity into a left string.
  Put the other wide quantity into a right string.
  Put "" into a combined string.
  Put 0 into a carry number.
  Put the left string's length into a remaining number.
  If the right string's length is greater than the remaining number,
    put the right string's length into the remaining number.
  Loop.
    If the remaining number is 0, break.
    Take the last decimal digit from the left string into a left digit number.
    Take the last decimal digit from the right string into a right digit number.
    Put the left digit number plus the right digit number plus the carry number into a column number.
    Divide the column number by 10 giving a quotient number and a remainder number.
    Put the quotient number into the carry number.
    Prepend the remainder number to the combined string.
    Subtract 1 from the remaining number.
  Repeat.
  If the carry number is not 0, prepend the carry number to the combined string.
  Put the combined string into the other wide quantity.
  Normalize the other wide quantity.

\ Subtract the first from the second. Precondition: second >= first.
\ Callers of this routine must check the order before subtracting.
To subtract a wide quantity from another wide quantity:
  Put the wide quantity into a smaller string.
  Put the other wide quantity into a larger string.
  Put "" into a difference string.
  Put 0 into a borrow number.
  Put the larger string's length into a remaining number.
  Loop.
    If the remaining number is 0, break.
    Take the last decimal digit from the larger string into a large digit number.
    Take the last decimal digit from the smaller string into a small digit number.
    Put the large digit number minus the small digit number minus the borrow number into a difference digit number.
    Put 0 into the borrow number.
    If the difference digit number is less than 0,
      add 10 to the difference digit number; put 1 into the borrow number.
    Prepend the difference digit number to the difference string.
    Subtract 1 from the remaining number.
  Repeat.
  Put the difference string into the other wide quantity.
  Normalize the other wide quantity.

\ Multiplication by one decimal digit never needs a large intermediate.
\ The digit parameter must be from 0 to 9.
To scale a wide quantity by a decimal digit number:
  Put the wide quantity into a remaining string.
  Put "" into a product string.
  Put 0 into a carry number.
  Loop.
    If the remaining string is blank, break.
    Take the last decimal digit from the remaining string into a digit number.
    Put the digit number times the decimal digit number plus the carry number into a column number.
    Divide the column number by 10 giving a quotient number and a remainder number.
    Put the quotient number into the carry number.
    Prepend the remainder number to the product string.
  Repeat.
  If the carry number is not 0, prepend the carry number to the product string.
  Put the product string into the wide quantity.
  Normalize the wide quantity.

\ Add place-value zeroes after a number (multiplication by a power of ten).
To append decimal zeroes to a wide quantity given a number:
  Privatize the number.
  Loop.
    If the number is less than 1, break.
    Append "0" to the wide quantity.
    Subtract 1 from the number.
  Repeat.
  Normalize the wide quantity.

\ Schoolbook multiplication; the second operand receives the result.
\ A separate helper performs the inner digit multiplication, so the
\ compiler never needs to handle a loop inside another loop.
To multiply a wide quantity by another wide quantity:
  Put the wide quantity into a multiplicand wide quantity.
  Put the other wide quantity into a remaining string.
  Put "0" into a product wide quantity.
  Put 0 into a position number.
  Loop.
    If the remaining string is blank, break.
    Take the last decimal digit from the remaining string into a digit number.
    Put the multiplicand wide quantity into a partial wide quantity.
    Scale the partial wide quantity by the digit number.
    Append decimal zeroes to the partial wide quantity given the position number.
    Add the partial wide quantity to the product wide quantity.
    Add 1 to the position number.
  Repeat.
  Put the product wide quantity into the other wide quantity.
  Normalize the other wide quantity.

\ Keep subtracting a divisor while a partial remainder is large enough.
\ The long-division caller guarantees that this takes no more than 9 steps.
To remove copies of a wide quantity from another wide quantity:
  Loop.
    If the other wide quantity is less than the wide quantity, break.
    Subtract the wide quantity from the other wide quantity.
  Repeat.

\ Long division, one decimal digit at a time. The first operand
\ receives the remainder, in the range from zero to divisor minus one.
\ Precondition: divisor is positive and both numbers are normalized.
To reduce a wide quantity modulo another wide quantity:
  Put the wide quantity into a source string.
  Put "0" into a remainder wide quantity.
  Slap a substring on the source string.
  Loop.
    If the substring is blank, break.
    Append the substring's first's target to the remainder wide quantity.
    Normalize the remainder wide quantity.
    Remove copies of the other wide quantity from the remainder wide quantity.
    Add 1 to the substring's first.
  Repeat.
  Put the remainder wide quantity into the wide quantity.
  Normalize the wide quantity.


\ The calendar's SAVE rule is not the ordinary remainder.
\ With M = 2^127 - 1, every output must be in the range 1 through M.
\ For nonnegative input: reduce modulo M, then replace zero with M.
\ Signed modular subtraction is a separate future requirement.
To keep a wide quantity for the sauce:
  Put "170141183460469231731687303715884105727" into a limit wide quantity.
  Reduce the wide quantity modulo the limit wide quantity.
  If the wide quantity is "0", put the limit wide quantity into the wide quantity.


\ Both operands here must already be in the saved range 1..M.
\ These two routines implement SAVE(a-b), including negative differences,
\ without ever storing a negative decimal string.
\ The first is the number being subtracted; the second receives the answer.
To subtract a wide quantity from another wide quantity with sauce wrapping:
  If the other wide quantity is less than the wide quantity,
    wrap the sauce difference given the wide quantity and the other wide quantity; exit.
  Subtract the wide quantity from the other wide quantity.
  Keep the other wide quantity for the sauce.

\ If the second operand b is smaller than the first operand a,
\ then SAVE(b-a) is M-(a-b), since both operands are in 1..M.
To wrap the sauce difference given a wide quantity and another wide quantity:
  Put the wide quantity into a difference wide quantity.
  Subtract the other wide quantity from the difference wide quantity.
  Put "170141183460469231731687303715884105727" into a wrapped wide quantity.
  Subtract the difference wide quantity from the wrapped wide quantity.
  Put the wrapped wide quantity into the other wide quantity.


\ These helpers accept ordinary nonnegative CAL numbers as multipliers.
\ A wide quantity is never converted to a CAL number.
To multiply a wide quantity by a number:
  Convert the number to a string.
  Put the string into a factor wide quantity.
  Multiply the factor wide quantity by the wide quantity.

To add a number to a wide quantity:
  Convert the number to a string.
  Put the string into an added wide quantity.
  Add the added wide quantity to the wide quantity.

\ Add factor times first value to the second value.
To add a weighted wide quantity to another wide quantity given a number:
  Put the wide quantity into a weighted wide quantity.
  Multiply the weighted wide quantity by the number.
  Add the weighted wide quantity to the other wide quantity.
